# SYNOPSIS

```usage
xargs [-0prtx] [-E eofstr] [-n num] [-P maxprocs] [-s num] [cmd [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`xargs` reads arguments from standard input and runs <cmd>, with the <arg>s first, then as many of the arguments read as fit, again and again until the input is used up.
Without <cmd> it runs `/bin/echo`.
With no input it runs nothing.

Arguments are separated by spaces, tabs and newlines.
A `'` or `"` quotes up to the next of the same, newlines inside dropped; a `\` takes the next character as it is.

A command line holds at most the C library's `ARG_MAX` less 4096 bytes, and 9999 arguments.
<cmd> is found through `$PATH`.

: `-0`
  Separate arguments by NUL bytes alone, and do no quoting.
: `-E` <eofstr>
  Take an argument equal to <eofstr> as the end of the input.
: `-I` <replstr>
  Run <cmd> once for each line read, replacing each <replstr> in the <arg>s by the line.
  A line's blanks are kept, but not its leading ones.
  Implies `-x` and `-n 1`.
: `-n` <num>
  Use at most <num> arguments read for each command.
: `-P` <maxprocs>
  Run up to <maxprocs> commands at once.
: `-p`
  Ask before each command: write it to standard error, then read an answer from `/dev/tty`; a line starting `y` or `Y` runs it.
: `-r`
  Do not run <cmd> when there is no input; as `xargs` never does, this changes nothing.
: `-s` <num>
  Make each command line at most <num> bytes.
: `-t`
  Write each command to standard error before running it.
: `-x`
  Stop if an argument does not fit the command line.

`xargs` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/xargs`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/xargs`.
There is no native `xargs`.

# EXAMPLES

sbase's test (`tests/posix/sbasetest.c`) runs `xargs -n 1 echo` with `a b c` on standard input, and gets:

```text
a
b
c
```

